# submission_header_checks — applied via the `submission-cleanup` service in
# master.cf for authenticated submissions only (ports 465 + 587).  Inbound
# MX traffic still uses the default `cleanup` and is NOT affected by this
# file, so spam-filter Received-path checks on inbound mail stay intact.
#
# Goal: prevent locally-authenticated outbound mail from leaking the
# submitting user's home IP, reverse DNS, or MUA-added X-Originating-IP.
# Postfix's outbound `smtp` client adds a fresh single-line Received: when
# it relays the queued message to the recipient's MX — so the recipient
# still sees a Received chain, but it starts at *this server* rather than
# at the user's residential IP.
#
# Rule format: PCRE — postfix matches each logical header against every
# pattern, top-down.  `IGNORE` silently drops the matched header.  Patterns
# are anchored at start-of-header (the leading `^` is implicit but added
# here for readability).

# 1. Drop the client-side Received header.  Postfix's authenticated
#    submission service tags its own Received with the ESMTPSA keyword
#    (RFC 3848 §4: "SMTP Authentication" → "ESMTPSA").  Matching on that
#    keyword is safer than `^Received:.*from` because it only deletes
#    Receiveds *we* generated for SASL submissions; relay-injected
#    Receiveds from other hops (none for submission, but defence in
#    depth) keep their normal form `ESMTP` / `ESMTPS` and are untouched.
/^Received:.*\bwith ESMTPSA\b/   IGNORE

# 2. Drop X-Originating-IP if the MUA added one (Outlook, some Webmails).
/^X-Originating-IP:/             IGNORE

# 3. Drop any client-leaked X-Forwarded-For chain.
/^X-Forwarded-For:/              IGNORE
